Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chairman. I have to start with a bit of irony. The UN was founded to be a place where the world can come together to talk. And, what most of us do all day is communicate.
So you’d imagine that our premier resolution on communications and the founding charter of the Department of Global Communications would be one of our best.
You’d imagine that it plainly lays out how we convey the UN mission to our capitals and to the world.
It does not. Worse—it’s 30 pages of resolution soup that most people out in the real world would have trouble finishing or even understanding. Our team ran an analysis on the resolution and found that it is at least 30% redundant—meaning it just repeats the same tortured prose that it did on page 2 and again on page 29.
Sentences that obscure rather than inform—that make it less clear what the UN’s mission is and how we should convey it—are not better just because we repeat them or cite enough quote “recollections” of prior resolutions.
I think the problem here is deeper and also fully fixable. The UN has grown comfortable talking to itself, but really doesn’t know how to talk about itself.
Colleagues, we, all of us, have a great story to tell about our work here. Sharing the UN’s mission with the world should be like selling water in a drought. Who doesn’t want what the UN aspires to do best: peace, security, and lifesaving humanitarian aid?
It’s when we start to drift—when the UN takes on a cornucopia of issues that it doesn’t need to worry about—that we poison the good fruits of our labor.
This resolution is perhaps the perfect example of this mission creep. Its language is layered in to divide rather than unite, to make subtle ideological pronouncements from the UN that are solely Member States’ prerogatives.
The United States will always defend freedom of expression at home and abroad. We will not accept efforts, like those in this resolution, to restrict that freedom under the banner of “disinformation” or “misinformation.” The public must be informed to the teeth but never controlled.
The continued irony of this resolution is that it talks a big game about misinformation and disinformation, but then includes references that do little more than shield certain governments from scrutiny over their own governance and human rights records.
And then there is the familiar list—Sustainable Development Goals, climate change, gender—inserted here not because they clarify the mandates of the Department of Global Communications, but because they have become this building’s default talking points.
When everything is a priority, nothing is. And, we are poor communicators because of it. You wonder why everyday people around the world are confused about what we do here or even hate our work? Because of this.
We can do better. Indeed, we must do better. The UN has a sterling message to share with the world. Right now, its best communicators are hamstrung, because they’ve got this darn resolution to read, and no one knows what it actually means for them.
If we’re serious about reform—and the American people and our friends here at the UN are absolutely serious about reform—then let’s start with this resolution. The United Nations should communicate on what is actually in this mandate—clearly, directly, and with purpose.
We’ll get there if we start from scratch. 30 pages of gobbledygook are not helpful for anyone. We welcome the biennialization of this process, but we also believe that this Committee should get back to basics now. Over the next two years, we will build a concise, disciplined resolution from scratch, from a blank page, and fill it with our best ideas.
At the next session, the United States looks forward to working with partners who are ready to produce a focused, disciplined, and apolitical resolution—one that gets the UN back to basics. We really can communicate clearly, operate effectively, and deliver results.
The people that we are here to serve demand it and need it. And our people—and this institution’s potential—deserve it. Thank you.
