Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you SRSG Ruiz Massieu and Special Representative Christofides for their briefings today. I also want to thank Haiti’s Prime Minster Fils-Aime and our U.S. Ambassador to the Organization of American States Lee Rizzuto for joining us today.
The United States is pleased to see the completion of the Transitional Presidential Council mandate and the successful transfer of power to Prime Minister Fils-Aime and his Council of Ministers.
As noted in the Secretary-General’s report, terrorist gangs continue to perpetuate violence against the Haitian people, including rape, ransom, robbery, and murder, while operations to fight back against terrorist gangs intensify, showing measurable, if fragile, progress.
To meet Haiti’s call for assistance in countering gang violence, this Council authorized the Gang Suppression Force (GSF) last September to counter the gangs terrorizing the Haitian people and destabilizing the Western Hemisphere.
We are not measuring success by what international forces can achieve in Haiti. We are measuring success by how quickly Haiti will no longer need them.
The GSF is designed to work in support of – not in place of – Haitian institutions. It is designed to create space for Haitian forces to expand, professionalize, and ultimately assume responsibility. That transition has begun.
It is worth reminding that the GSF, with both military and police units, will grow to 5,500 personnel conducting intelligence-led operations and securing critical infrastructure. And it will operate under a clear command structure consistent with a mandate to neutralize, isolate, and deter gangs.
The GSF will also benefit from a UN logistics backbone through an innovative hybrid mission model. Altogether, this represents a formidable combination to accelerate baseline security and shape conditions to improve Haiti’s long-term stability.
Since September, the Standing Group of Partners to the GSF and the UN have moved rapidly to stand up the GSF and the UN Support Office in Haiti. This month marks several important milestones:
First, GSF Special Representative Christofides and his initial staff are on the ground, launching the civilian staff’s critical leadership, oversight, and support functions. We’re pleased the Council heard directly from the Special Representative today.
Second, the UN Support Office in Haiti assumed responsibility for the GSF with the logistical support needed to carry out its mandate.
And third, additional troop- and police-contributing countries are arriving in Port-au-Prince, building through sustained deployments to reach its full authorized force.
We are grateful to Kenya for its extended leadership of the MSS mission, as well as to Guatemala, El Salvador, Jamaica, Belize, and The Bahamas for their service.
As we know, stability cannot be won through security forces alone. This GSF mandate addresses the security crisis threatening the sovereignty of the Haitian state. But we break the cycle of violence by pairing credible offramps with real economic opportunity – so when the guns fall silent, jobs may follow.
We call on the international community to help us win peace for the long haul – injecting sorely needed investments through capital, infrastructure, and job creation to keep impoverished next-generation leaders from returning to the deadly path of gang violence.
Responsible governance is also critical for Haiti’s long-term stability. We note the Provisional Electoral Council’s finalization of the operational plan for elections. We must work to help Haiti return to elected governance. The United States welcomes BINUH’s work to ensure alignment on electoral assistance among international partners to support a coherent and efficient process.
We are pleased with the reconstitution of Haiti’s National Commission for Disarmament, Dismantlement, and Reintegration. We applaud BINUH’s work to support its efforts to take concrete measures to implement a coherent plan expeditiously. As areas are cleared and held, Haiti’s government can focus on providing essential services to the people, who will recover as quickly as the economy can rebuild. International support remains crucial to this imperative.
We note and will review the Secretary-General’s forthcoming report on a range of options and costs related to the implementation of a Haitian-led DDR program, as well as justice reform as mandated by Resolution 2814.
The United States stands with Haiti and will continue working with Haitians and international partners towards the stabilization of the country.
Thank you.
