Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you to the SRSG for her briefing.
The United States continues to support efforts to advance a Libyan-led political process toward unified governance that creates the necessary conditions for successful national elections.
We encourage all Libyan and external stakeholders to engage constructively on the SRSG’s political roadmap. Real progress on unifying political, economic, and military institutions is essential to achieve the stability and prosperity that the Libyan people deserve.
In support of Libyan leaders and UNSMIL’s roadmap, the United States is bringing Libya’s eastern and western stakeholders together to forge concrete progress toward economic and military integration.
This includes the FLINTLOCK multinational special operations exercise that began last week. Libyan professional military officers from the east and west are jointly participating in border security and counterterrorism training led by the United States and international partners.
Libya’s leadership in hosting a portion of the exercise marks a historic milestone. By facilitating the training of over ten nations, Libya’s joint forces are highlighting their commitment to regional security and demonstrating the strength of military unity.
Earlier this month, the United States also played a leading role in bringing together Libyan stakeholders to agree on the country’s first unified, national budget in more than 13 years.
Full implementation of the unified budget will help to advance Libya’s financial stability, enable the transparent implementation of development projects across Libya, and strengthen Libya’s vital technocratic institutions, as well as improve the livelihoods of the Libyan people.
We hope this momentum continues and encourages Libyan stakeholders to take further steps to unify their institutions.
As the Secretary-General’s report notes, increasing divisions and the politicization of the judicial system risk undermining a key pillar of the Libyan state. The United States supports Libyan efforts to unify its institutions, including the upholding of the independence, integrity, and unity of the judiciary.
On sanctions, we welcome the Security Council’s adoption of 2819, which maintains the arms embargo and protects Libya’s frozen assets for the future benefit of the Libyan people.
This resolution called for payments for crude oil exported from Libya to be routed exclusively through accounts of the National Oil Corporation at the Libyan Foreign Bank. This impedes illicit exports of petroleum and avoids the diversion of energy revenues from the Libyan state and the Libyan people.
This resolution also renewed the mandate for the UN Panel of Experts on Libya. The Panel briefed the sanctions committee on its 2025 final report, which has just been published.
We express concern about the alleged efforts to divert energy revenues from the Libyan state. We must keep working with Libyan stakeholders to safeguard Libya’s energy revenue.
The unification of political, economic, and military institutions protects against attempts to exploit the country’s divisions and is key to Libya’s sovereignty, stability, and long-term prosperity.
Thank you.
