Ambassador Tammy Bruce
Deputy Representative of the United States to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. And thank you to today’s briefers.
Colleagues, President Trump recognized early that the change in Syria represented an opportunity to be seized. At a moment of turmoil across much of the region, Syria’s story stands apart.
Syria is in a different place than a year and a half ago. The Syrian government is now a partner in defeating ISIS, and it is preventing destabilizing activities by Hizballah and other Iranian aligned terror groups.
UN member states must take advantage of this opportunity and support the Syrian government’s counterterrorism capabilities. They should engage with the Syrian government regarding potential repatriation or removal of nationals who were or remain in displaced persons camps and detention facilities in northeast Syria.
From the regional perspective, we now see Syria working with Lebanon to maintain calm on their border during this period of instability, with Jordan to help stem the flow of illicit drugs, and with Iraq to transport oil overland to the Mediterranean and ease energy supply shortages caused by Iran’s aggression against Gulf states.
In a similar vein, we hope high-level talks between Syria and Israel will resume soon.
Looking to Syria’s northeast, the United States continues to support implementation of the January agreements on military and civilian reintegration, and we commend the Syrian government and the Syrian Democratic Forces for their significant progress to date.
Looking to the south, we urge further steps to implement the November 2025 Suwayda Roadmap. We note with concern reports of potential war crimes and crimes against humanity committed by all sides during last summer’s violence. We call on all parties to hold perpetrators to account.
Transitional justice and accountability are critical to preventing recurring cycles of violence and to the protection of minority factions. We encourage continued progress toward justice for crimes committed under Assad and after the fall of Assad.
On the humanitarian situation, we are concerned by the influx of refugees and returnees from Lebanon into Syria, and by the humanitarian impact of flooding in the northeast. We commend the cooperation between the UN and the Syrian government as they respond, and we urge member states to offer support.
With continued U.S. and UN support for the Syrian people and their government, we believe Syria will take its rightful place as an anchor of stability in a turbulent region.
Thank you.
