Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President, and thank you to all of our briefers today.
The United States holds to a simple principal: When conditions change, peacekeeping missions need to adapt or close.
The operations authorized by this Council—which risk lives and significant taxpayer funding from all of our countries—are meant to be a temporary tool. Their aim is simple: help host governments navigate peace and security crises when they can’t help themselves.
Unfortunately, more times than those on this Council would like to admit, missions drift from temporary solutions into open-ended commitments—burning through billions of dollars and risking peacekeepers’ lives—while some host governments game the system and never hold up their side of the bargain.
UNISFA exemplifies this problem. It’s a supposedly “interim” force which has consumed billions of dollars over its 15 years in place—with little to show for in lasting political progress.
In fact, during UNIFSA’s tenure, the governments of Sudan and South Sudan have made virtually no progress toward delineating the border between Sudan and South Sudan, the demilitarization of Abyei, or deciding the area’s final status.
Worse, both governments actively undermine the effectiveness through deliberate acts of obstruction disguised as bureaucratic delays. Most recently, they have resorted to blocking the appointment of a civilian deputy head of mission and denying visas for the mission’s people and police.
Sudan and South Sudan must unequivocally demonstrate progress on these benchmarks outlined in the mandate if they want to see this mission renewed another year. Their actions are speaking louder than their words.
Peacekeeping missions have a clear job to do: fulfill their mandates. Our peacekeepers should always be working themselves out of a job. Appeasing host governments to maintain ambiguous consent is not their job. Consent cannot become a shield for inaction and de facto renewal.
Council members and regional partners must do more to encourage full cooperation by host governments and conflict parties, not only when mandate negotiations and press releases come due.
None of us should be satisfied when missions adapt only in order to survive another year. Survival should not be our threshold for success. Adaptation must be innovative. They should actually advance a mission’s mandate and, ultimately, make forward progress toward transition, closure, and peace.
MINUSCA is a rare example of a current mission that has collectively moved towards a broader strategic goal. It is long past time the UN captures and codifies this kind of integrated strategic planning across the entire peacekeeping enterprise.
We welcome MINUSCA’s repositioning of forces following the successful elections last December. We encourage adjustments to MINUSCA’s force laydown to economize its footprint in more stable areas, including Bangui, and transition responsibility to the Central African Republic police or national security forces.
For too long this Council has watched idly as host governments strangle mission operations, as peace processes stall or backslide, and as mandates become impossible to fulfill. We have seen missions blocked, restricted, and ignored—yet these missions are renewed anyway.
And despite these glaring realities, this Council has time and time again renewed failing missions with only minimal changes to mandates.
We simply cannot continue to call for “adaptive peacekeeping” while opting for the status quo out of sheer habit. If we are serious about peacekeeping and reform, renewal must be earned—never assumed.
Thank you.
