AS DELIVERED
For The United States remains steadfast in our commitment to protecting human dignity, strengthening families, and improving maternal health outcomes through practical, evidence-based solutions.
As we have made clear, there is no one-size-fits-all approach to population policy. We stand with our international partners in affirming that policies must first respect national sovereignty and the right of every nation to make its own laws, especially as it relates to protecting life and supporting families. Ideological agendas should never be imposed on sovereign nations.
Strong families are the cornerstone of strong societies. The United States reaffirms that the family is the foundational unit of society from which health, support, care, and human wellbeing flourish. We are committed to advancing policies that create environments in which children and families can thrive, where marriage is celebrated, and parents are empowered.
Across many countries, including our own, declining fertility and marriage rates, delayed family formation, lack of support for mothers and fathers, and rapidly aging populations are reshaping societies. These developments carry serious economic and social implications and underscore the need for policies that support parents and family formation.
Technology can be a powerful tool in addressing these challenges. Under President Trump’s leadership, we are pursuing a pro-family, pro-health agenda that prioritizes the well-being and future of our children and our nation. Through our efforts to Make America Healthy Again, we are addressing the declines in fertility and family formation across all aspects of life and we recognize the strong connections between public health and a healthy environment.
The Trump Administration acknowledges that strong families depend on healthy communities. We should all have the shared goal of creating nations where mothers and fathers can raise thriving, healthy families.
Innovations in telehealth, AI, and medical research are expanding access to care and improving maternal and child health outcomes. Advancements in technology have facilitated incredible progress in healthcare and society, and can continue to do so if we avoid innovation-limiting overregulation of technologies.
The United States recognizes the growing challenge of screen time and its effects on youth mental health. HHS is leading a national initiative to raise awareness and empower parents, ensuring that families are equipped to navigate the complexities of the digital age.
We believe parents hold an irreplaceable role in safeguarding their children, and the role of the government is to create policies that empower and support them in that mission.
As the Commission on Population and Development, let us not forget that continued development across all populations depends not only on innovation, but on supporting the foundational institutions that have allowed societies to thrive throughout human history. Families and communities that are supported by their leaders, are what holds societies together.
