Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. We heard a lot today. We heard from the ambassador from Iran a lot about their issues with this ongoing conflict with the U.S.
But colleagues, that’s not what this resolution is about today. This is not about Iran in the U.S. today. This is about Iran and the Gulf Cooperation Council and Bahrain and the world.
And here we also heard about some dangerous precedents. But here’s what I want the world to understand in terms of the dangerous precedent that was just set today by opposing this good faith effort from the Kingdom of Bahrain and the Gulf countries to ensure their shipping can flow freely.
One can only imagine a future conflict where a party to the conflict decides it’s going to impose collective punishment on the entire world and then use that as leverage in the conflict. One can only imagine a situation where a party decides to shut down the Straits of Gibraltar or the Bering Strait or the Malacca Strait to further its ends. One can only imagine conflicts where a party decides to hold the rest of the world hostage because of a dispute.
And colleagues, this is a situation the world cannot and should not live with, that the Gulf Arabs cannot tolerate. Freedom of navigation is fundamental to a nation’s sovereignty and survival.
So Mr. President, the United States will support all measures for your country and for those affected to ensure it has the freedom of navigation, the freedom of transit in line with international law.
So my message to the Iranian regime is, as people go hungry and as economies suffer, it is squarely on your shoulders.
I thank you, Mr. President.
