Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative to the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. Thank you, Secretary-General Albudaiwi – good to see you – and Assistant Secretary-General Khiari for their briefings. And again, welcome, Foreign Minister Al Zayani. And congratulations to Bahrain on the start of its Council presidency.
Colleagues, President Trump said during his visit to Riyadh last year in his first overseas visit of his second term, that the nations of the Gulf Cooperation Council are at the forefront, are leading in creating a stable, peaceful, and prosperous Middle East.
On a personal note, when I served as a soldier, I fought alongside soldiers from your countries. We came under fire together, we fought together, and we defeated extremism together. But there was one thing that my GCC colleagues could do better than me or any of my other colleagues with us, and that was to make people believe in a better future.
I will never forget a captain from the United Arab Emirates, who would talk to and often convince Afghans to reject the extremism of ISIS and al-Qa’ida. He held out the promise of prosperity, of education, of religious tolerance. He would point to these great cities that I’ve now had the honor to visit in my life: of Riyadh, of Doha, Dubai, Manama, and Kuwait City – each of them welcome shores for tourism, for business, and for education. And he pointed importantly to these Afghans stuck in a cycle of violence and poverty to a better life for their next generation, with a religious and a cultural authority that no westerner could do.
And so for 45 years, the GCC has helped anchor regional stability in one of the most strategically consequential parts of the world, where the arteries of commerce, and energy, and national security run right through their backyards.
And its members have never closed their ports, or waterways, or commerce lanes to our ships. To the contrary, since its founding in 1981, GCC members have worked to preserve order through decades of conflict, extremism, and external aggression – because they have sown the seeds of stability, the Gulf has become one of the great engines of global economic growth, trade, investment, and innovation.
And so as we work now to negotiate and implement President Trump’s Comprehensive Peace Plan to finally, hopefully, prayerfully end the Gaza Conflict and adopt Resolution 2803 that authorized the Board of Peace and the International Stabilization Force, and the National Committee for the Administration of Gaza, our GCC colleagues were there. They were there to build support and to contribute to its successful launch. And have now engaged and are contributing their time and their treasure to the Board of Peace and to actually implement the steps necessary to resolve that conflict.
Additionally, when President Trump made history with his announcement of U.S. sanctions relief for the Syrian people, with the new Government of Syria, we worked with this Council to provide sanctions relief, but yet again it was the GCC who worked with us to reintegrate Syria into the region and to supercharge its economic recovery.
And now, as we speak, even as Iran has continued its indiscriminate attacks against their cities, their neighborhoods, their hotels, their resorts, international airports, civilian infrastructure, we have seen our Gulf partners rise to the occasion, and I want to personally thank them and thank them on behalf of the United States. They are rising to the occasion in the region, and they are rising to the occasion here at the Security Council.
We too long for the coming days where we once again hear the sounds in your streets that ring out with racecars from Formula 1, not F-15s and certainly not Shahed drones.
We are grateful for Bahrain’s achievement in pushing forward the historic Resolution 2817, with the most co-sponsors of any Security Council resolution in UN history, and for its continued efforts to hold the Iranian regime to account.
Colleagues, we’ve heard a lot of comments today about securing shipping lanes, about condemning Iran’s attacks, about stopping its atrocious attacks on civilian shipping and other forms of infrastructure. That I would call on you to further those statements and those speeches with real help to Bahrain and GCC countries in doing something about it.
No country, certainly not a genocidal Iranian regime that massacres its own people and has spent decades sowing instability and violence around the world, no country should be able to hold the world’s economies hostage to try to gain leverage in a dispute.
So colleagues, the bottom line is this: I believe, we believe, the United States believes the GCC can teach this body much about what it means to get back to basics. And the bottom line is that our allies and partners in the Gulf Cooperation Council can count on the United States to march alongside you for a better future – both here at this Council, and in the region. Thank you.
